Title: The Winter Ball
Credit: Written by
Author: Previz Stage
Draft date: 2026-10-24

INT. GRAND BALLROOM - NIGHT

A gilded ballroom in 1890s Vienna. Crystal chandeliers blaze over a parquet floor, tall mirrors between marble columns, velvet drapes drawn against the snow. An orchestra's chairs stand empty on a low stage.

ELISABETH VON HALLER (20s, upright, chestnut hair pinned high, an ivory silk ball gown with long gloves, a sapphire at her throat) stands alone beneath the central chandelier.

CAPTAIN LUKAS BRANDT (30s, tall, dark moustache, a white dress uniform with gold braid and a sabre) approaches from the doors and bows.

BRANDT
Your father has asked me to escort you home.

ELISABETH
My father has asked you to do a great many things, Captain.

BRANDT
He is concerned for your reputation.

ELISABETH
(turning away)
He is concerned for his.

EXT. PALACE COURTYARD - DAWN

Snow falls softly on a cobbled courtyard. Gas lamps still burn along the colonnade, their halos pale against the first grey light. A carriage, its horses stamping in the cold, waits by the gate.

Elisabeth descends the steps in a fur-lined cloak. Brandt follows, holding her forgotten gloves.

BRANDT
You left these.

ELISABETH
I left a great deal more than that.

BRANDT
Then let me carry some of it.

ELISABETH
(taking the gloves)
Not tonight, Captain. Perhaps not ever.
